  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 202 PM CST Thu Nov 12 2026\n\nFriday Night through Thursday:\n\nNo major changes to the extended period with this forecast update.\n\nA large cutoff 500mb trough will continue eastward toward the \nAtlantic Coast Friday night into Saturday. Canadian high pressure \nwill settle into the upper Great Lakes and western Corn Belt Region \nFriday night. A colder air mass, radiational cooling and light winds \nwill allow overnight lows to drop into the low to mid 20s away from \nLake Michigan. Some lingering elevated winds and scattered cloud \ncover along the Lake Michigan shoreline Friday night will allow for \nlows to stay in the upper 20s to near 30\n\nSaturday, 850mb winds will continue to come around to northerly and \nnorthwesterly through the day into the nighttime hours. This will \npush a lingering lake effect band over Lake Michigan away from the \nWisconsin shoreline, keeping us dry. High pressure should support \nweakening winds through Saturday and a mix of sun and clouds.\n\nHigh pressure is then expected to linger through the extended period \nkeeping the weather sunny, dry, and quiet. There will be a muted \nwarming trend, with highs around normal through Monday, and then in \nthe low to mid 50s by Tuesday through Thursday as some modest upper \nlevel ridging tries to develop over the central plains. Some low \nrain chances (~30%) return Thursday morning as a weak trough passes \nover the state.\n\nCMiller",
